out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Yara has to write to the agent for the analyser reagents and has been putting it off for eleven days, and it is not the English: she knows exactly what she wants to know and did not know how to ask it without it sounding like an accusation. Three things. Whether the lot that came in on the fourth of November is from the batch recalled in September in Lebanon. Who at their end signed the release note, because the signature is initials and she cannot read them. And why the box says two to eight degrees when the insert says two to twenty-five. Omar said those are three reasonable questions, and she agreed — and then read out the second as she would have written it in April: "Who signed this? I cannot read the signature." A reasonable question that reads as the first line of a complaint about a person, and she wants initials, not a complaint about a person. What she has written is: "I should be grateful if you could tell me who signed the release note for lot 4471, as the initials are not legible on our copy." The same question, nobody on trial — and "our copy" does half of it, because it allows the problem to be at her end. The temperature one frightened her most, because somebody has made a mistake and it might be them and it might be the printer: "Could you tell me which of the two storage temperatures on lot 4471 is the correct one?" She has not said either is wrong; she has asked which is right, and whoever reads it will find out which is wrong on her behalf. Ms Huda said an indirect question buys three things — it takes the accusation out, it turns a question about a person into a question about a document, and it leaves room for the answer to be that you are wrong, which is the only reason anybody answers these letters at all.']));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('✗ Could you tell me when did it arrive? → ✓ **when it arrived**.   ✗ I should like to know who did sign it. → ✓ **who signed it**.   ✗ Could you tell me that the lot was recorded? → ✓ **whether**.   ✗ Could you tell me is it correct? → ✓ **whether it is correct**.   ✗ I would be grateful if you can tell me. → ✓ if you **could** / if you **would**.'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** Because the letter is going to a company that receives complaints all day, and a reader who thinks they are reading a complaint spends the first paragraph of the reply establishing that nothing is their fault. She says it twice so that there is no ambiguity in either the first line or the last.'));
out.push(A('**2** A printing error at a factory; her own photocopier; and her own misreading of a withdrawal notice. Two of the three are hers.'));
out.push(A('**3** The third, by 11 December — because it is the only one of the three that changes what the laboratory does tomorrow morning. The other two can wait until the new year.'));
out.push(A('Two further moves worth naming. _We have stored it at 2–8 °C, which is the stricter of the two, so nothing has been lost either way_ — she has already done the cautious thing, which takes the urgency off the reader and leaves it with her. And the last paragraph, where she **states** rather than asks, and says why: _so that your answer does not have to begin by establishing it._ That sentence saves the reader a paragraph and saves her a week.'));
out.push(S('Stage 6 · Write with help (sample)'));
