out.push(S('Stage 7P · Respond (sample slip — criterion named)'));
out.push(A('_Three sentences, each naming its criterion:_ **"On the term being doable: five words in a specification he is redrafting anyway is the smallest term I have seen in this room, and it is the only reason the letter will work. On the not-a-failure line: it is there and it is the right length — one sentence — and I would resist the temptation to explain it further. On the absence of a knife: there is none now, but the line about telling your own management with his name on it is doing something, and I cannot tell whether it is generosity or leverage; read it aloud to somebody and see which they hear."**'));
out.push(S('Stage 7R · Share'));
out.push(A('Count the people who found out what the other side was able to do **before** accepting, and then count how many found out with **one telephone call**. Almost every acceptance in the room is being written in ignorance of the other person\'s powers, and in almost every case one indirect question would settle it in forty seconds — which is 19.3 arriving where it is actually needed, and it is worth saying that the two cycles are one cycle.'));
out.push(S('Homework (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['I made the call. I asked who signs off the night rota and was told that the pattern comes from the directorate but the allocation of people to nights is the ward manager\'s, entirely. So half of what I have been asking her for nine months was never hers to give and half of it was hers all along, and I did not know which half. She also said — and I did not ask this — that she has asked twice for a second person to be trained on the pumps and has been told there is no training budget, which means my term should not be about training at all. It should be forty minutes of my own time, unpaid, which nobody has to find a budget for, and I shall write that instead.']));

// ===================== 19.7 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('19.7  Review & Mediate: Who counts as a complainant'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Unit 19 quiz – corrections'));
out.push(A('**1** Could you tell me **when it arrived**?   **2** I have **had** the machine serviced twice.   **3** What they have offered **is** four units.   **4** Please reply **by** 20 December.   **5** I would be grateful if you **would** send it.   **6** I accept it **on** the understanding that it covers January.'));
out.push(A('Items **1** and **3** are the two that must not survive into Unit 20: ⚠ inversion after the opener, and ⚠ a plural verb after a _what_-clause. Item **5** is the one that will come back in every letter the class writes for the rest of the course.'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**b** Clause **1** puts her outside the procedure: "a complaint" means dissatisfaction received in writing **from a patient, a relative of a patient, or a member of the public**, and she is none of the three. Clause **9** is the one she can use: the complaints log is reviewed every quarter by the quality committee, and a body that reviews a log can be asked for **one figure** out of it.'));
out.push(A('The rest of the analysis is worth setting out, because it is the model for every procedure the class will ever read. Clause **2** attaches all the time limits to the word defined in clause 1, so a relative who writes about that trolley is owed a dated answer inside a month and a nurse of nine years is owed nothing. Clause **6** sends staff matters to a second procedure and clause **7** says it is at Appendix 2 — which nobody has seen, and which the ward clerk of eleven years has never heard of. **That is why there is no form for chasing equipment** (16.7), and the explanation is not malice or indifference: it is one word in clause 1 and a missing appendix.'));
out.push(NOTE('The mediation lesson is in Ms Huda\'s last line and it generalises: **a document that excludes you still contains something, and it is almost never the clause you are angry about — it is the one near the end, which nobody reads because by then the reader has stopped.** Make the class read every procedure from the **bottom** once, as an exercise. Reporting clauses, review clauses and audit clauses are where the usable requests are, because a body that reviews a log can be asked for a figure from it, and asking for a figure is not making a complaint.'));
out.push(A('And note what Rasha refuses to do, because somebody in the room will suggest it. She will not ask a patient\'s relative to write the letter for her. It would work. A complaint made through somebody who did not make it is a thing you cannot stand behind afterwards, and you will be asked to — and the person who would be asked first is the relative.'));
out.push(S('Language box – reading a complaints procedure'));
out.push(A('No exercises; it is the checklist for Stages 3 and 4. Five moves: **read clause one as a definition** (11.7) and find out whether you are inside it · **find where the excluded are sent, and whether it exists** · **separate the three explanations** (somebody lost it · nobody cares · the procedure is careful and excludes you by one word) and keep the third · **look for the usable clause near the end** · and **ask for one figure that is one line of arithmetic from a document they already hold**.'));
out.push(S('Stage 3 · Practise (sample – Procedure A, a college\'s appeals procedure)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  'Seven clauses. Clause one defines an appeal as a **written appeal by a registered student**, so a member of staff who thinks a mark is wrong has no standing at all under this document — and neither has a student who has finished the course, which is probably the commoner case.',
  'Clause four sends staff to the staff handbook, section 9. Clause five says section 9 is **under revision**. I have been "under revision" in two jobs and it has meant between four months and never.',
  'The usable clause is **seven**: appeal outcomes are reported annually to the senate. A body that receives an annual report can be asked for a figure out of it.',
  '**Our one figure, decided in two minutes.** How many appeals were upheld last year, out of how many heard. My partner wanted the number heard only; I wanted the proportion upheld, because a bare number of appeals tells you how many people complained and the proportion tells you whether complaining works. We did not agree and I think she is right that the bare number is easier to get.',
  'The clause I would write to the author about is **five** — one line, one fault: say when section 9 will be issued, or say that appeals by staff are heard under this procedure in the meantime. Not six faults; one.',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 3R · Perform – listener task'));
out.push(A('On Procedure A it is usually the number of appeals upheld; on Procedure B the number of enquiries answered outside five working days. In both cases the figure comes from the **last** clause, and in both cases the pairs reached it by giving up on the clause that excluded them — which is the move the cycle exists to teach and the one that does not come naturally to anybody.'));
out.push(S('Stage 4 · Your turn'));
out.push(A('Open — the student\'s own procedure. The three questions are the assessment: _are you inside the definition?_, _have you asked four people for the appendix?_ and _what is the one figure?_ Expect at least one student to find that they are outside their own organisation\'s procedure, and expect that to be the most useful forty minutes of the unit for them.'));
out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report – mediation (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Rasha found the procedure in a folder in the ward office on Monday and has read it about twenty times, and it explains something that has been happening to her since February. Clause one is the whole page: "a complaint" means dissatisfaction received in writing from a patient, from a relative of a patient, or from a member of the public — and she is none of those three. She has pushed that trolley for nine years and she cannot make a complaint about it, because she is not a kind of person who can make one. Clause two is the one that hurt: twenty working days, and everything on the page attaches to the word in clause one, so a relative of a patient who writes a letter about that trolley is owed an answer with a date on it inside a month, and she has had nine months and is owed nothing, and nobody has done anything wrong. Clause six sends her to the staff concerns procedure and clause seven says it is at Appendix 2; she has asked four people for Appendix 2 this week and nobody has seen it, and the ward clerk has been there eleven years and has never heard of it. Omar said so that is why there is no form for chasing equipment, and she said yes. In February she thought somebody had lost her trolley; in November she thought nobody cared; it is neither — the procedure is complete and careful and it has one word in clause one that leaves out every person who touches the equipment. Then the useful half. Clause nine: the log is reviewed every quarter by the quality committee. She cannot be a complainant and will not pretend to be one, and she will not ask a patient\'s relative to write a letter for her, which somebody suggested. But a committee that reviews a log every quarter can be asked for one figure, and asking for a figure is not making a complaint: how many items of equipment were in the workshop for more than ninety days last quarter. That is one line of arithmetic from a log they already have, nobody is accused of anything, and if the answer is larger than one, the trolley stops being her grievance and becomes their figure.']));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Exam practice – B1 Preliminary Reading Part 5'));
out.push(A('**1** B   **2** C   **3** D   **4** D   **5** C   **6** B'));
out.push(A('_Notes:_ **1** _take the trouble_ is the only collocation of the four. **2** _count as_ — ⚠ none of the other three takes _as_ here. **3** _depend **on**_ — a preposition item, and the only one in the set. **4** _dealt with_ — ⚠ the other three cannot be followed by _with_ in this sense. **5** _seen_ — _looked_ and _watched_ need an object or a preposition, and _noticed_ changes the meaning. **6** _before_ — ⚠ _until_ would reverse the sense of the sentence, which is the distractor the part is built on.'));
out.push(NOTE('Reading Part 5 is a six-gap four-option cloze and it is lost by reading for meaning rather than for the **word next to the gap**. Two habits worth the five minutes: read the words **immediately before and after** the gap first, because three of these six are decided by a preposition or a fixed partner (_take the trouble_, _count as_, _depend on_, _dealt with_); and when all four options mean roughly the same thing, the question is about collocation and not about sense, so say each option aloud with its neighbour and choose the one that sounds like something you have heard before.'));
out.push(S('Stage 6 · Write with help (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**TO THE QUALITY COMMITTEE · one figure from the complaints log** · R. Barakat, night sister, Ward 4 · December',
  'This is not a complaint and I am not able to make one: clause 1 of the complaints procedure defines a complainant as a patient, a relative of a patient, or a member of the public, and I am none of those.',
  '**What I am asking for.** One figure from a document you already hold. How many items of ward equipment were recorded in the workshop for more than ninety days, in the last quarter.',
  '**Why that figure.** Because Ward 4 has had one drug trolley since 2 February and I do not know whether that is unusual. If the answer is one, the matter is mine and I shall stop raising it. If the answer is eleven, it is not a ward matter at all. It is one line of arithmetic from the log named in clause 9, and nobody is accused of anything by it.',
  '**What I shall do with it.** Put it in the September report on the three-night blocks, which already contains the trolley as a limit on the figures, and send the committee a copy.',
  '**What I am not asking for.** I am not asking you to pursue the trolley, and I am not asking anybody to make a complaint on my behalf. I was advised to ask a patient\'s relative to write a letter for me, and I shall not.',
  '**And one thing about the procedure itself.** Clause 6 sends people like me to the staff concerns procedure, and clause 7 says it is at Appendix 2. I have asked four people for Appendix 2 this week, including a ward clerk of eleven years, and nobody has seen it or heard of it.',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 7 · Exam practice – B1 Preliminary Writing Part 1 (sample, 166 words)'));
out.push(A('Out of 20, **140 words or more**, and all **four** notes must be covered: what to do about the voucher in the first two lines; the three sentences in order; what to look for at the end of their letter and why; and the smaller new request with an example. A script that answers three of the four cannot reach the top band however well written.'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  'Dear Sam,',
  'Keep the voucher and say so in your first two lines. Thank them for it and say you will use it. A letter that accepts nothing is read as a refusal of everything, and then the rest of what you write is read as ingratitude rather than as a question.',
  'Then three sentences, in this order. **Accept:** _Thank you — I shall use the voucher._ **Distinguish:** _What I asked for, however, was not a voucher._ **Reopen:** _The question in my letter of the ninth is still open._ Leave out any one of the three and the letter fails — without the first you sound ungrateful, without the second you sound vague, and without the third the matter is closed.',
  'And look at the **last** sentence of their letter. It will say something like "we trust this resolves the matter". That sentence is not a courtesy: it is a claim, and if nobody answers it, it becomes true. Quote it and answer it in one line.',
  'Finally, make your new request **smaller** than the old one. Do not ask again for the explanation. Ask for the name of the person who could give it, by a date — a name can be supplied by whoever is holding your letter, and an explanation cannot.',
  'Let me know what they send,',
  'Lubna',
]));
out.push(NOTE('Writing Part 1 is an email with four notes and it is lost on **coverage** rather than on language. The same two habits every time: number the four notes in the margin and tick each as it is answered; and give each note **its own paragraph**, so that an examiner reading quickly can see all four. Here the fourth note asks for an example, and a script that gives the advice without the example has not covered it.'));
out.push(S('Unit 19 check  ·  A  Grammar and vocabulary'));
out.push(A('**1** c   **2** a   **3** a   **4** c   **5** a   **6** b   **7** c   **8** b'));
out.push(A('_Notes:_ **1** _whether_ — _that_ cannot introduce an indirect question. **4** a singular verb after a _what_-clause, every time. **5** _by_ a date; _within_ needs a period and _until_ reverses the sense. **6** _if you would_ — ⚠ _if you can_ is the taught error and _sent_ is a different conditional altogether. **7** no _do_-support and no inversion inside the clause.'));
out.push(S('Unit 19 check  ·  B  Listening (Part 3, note completion) – Track 19.15'));
out.push(A('**1** **writing**   **2** **public**   **3** **twenty**   **4** **Appendix**   **5** **date**   **6** **quarter**'));
out.push(A('Accept _in writing_ for 1, _the public_ for 2, _20_ for 3, _appendix_ for 4, _date of closure_ for 5, and _three months_ or _quarterly_ for 6. Gaps **3** and **6** are the two to replay: the extract names _three working days_ before it names _twenty_, and it names _eleven years_ and _four people_ in the same breath as _every quarter_, so candidates who write the first number they hear lose both.'));
out.push(NOTE('Listening Part 3 is note completion, and almost every mark lost is lost to a number heard in the wrong place. Three habits: read all six gaps in the pause and decide what **kind** of answer each needs (a word, a number, a name); write what you hear even if it looks wrong, and correct it on the second listening; and expect the recording to contain **more numbers than gaps** — this one contains seven numbers for three numerical gaps.'));
out.push(S('Unit 19 check  ·  C  Writing (sample, 178 words)'));
